<p class="lede">Carbon dioxide from students is a tracer gas you get for free. By logging CO₂ in a classroom you can estimate how much outdoor air it actually receives, either from the steady-state concentration during class or, more reliably, from how fast CO₂ falls after the students leave.</p>

<h2>What CO₂ can and cannot tell you</h2>
<p>CO₂ concentration indicates the balance between CO₂ generated by occupants and CO₂ removed by outdoor air. It is not a measure of contaminants in general and is not a health limit at typical classroom levels. Used carefully, it gives a field estimate of outdoor air per person or air changes per hour, which you can compare against the design ventilation rate.</p>
<p>The methods below verify ventilation. Using CO₂ to control outdoor air continuously is a separate topic covered in <a href="<?= url('guides/demand-controlled-ventilation-co2') ?>">demand-controlled ventilation with CO₂</a>.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Gather equipment and room data</h2>
<ol>
<li>Use a non-dispersive infrared (NDIR) CO₂ logger with a recent calibration and a stated accuracy of about ±50 ppm or better. Bring a second logger for outdoor readings.</li>
<li>Record room dimensions to calculate volume, number of occupants and their approximate ages, and the period schedule.</li>
<li>Note the air handler and damper status, and whether windows and doors are open during the test.</li>
<li>Get the design outdoor airflow for the room from the ventilation schedule or balancing report.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Step 2: Place the loggers</h2>
<ul>
<li>Mount the indoor logger at breathing height, about 3 to 5 ft (0.9 to 1.5 m), away from students' faces, supply diffusers, doors and windows.</li>
<li>Place a second logger outdoors near the air intake, or log outdoor CO₂ in the same period. Outdoor CO₂ commonly ranges around 400 to 450 ppm but can be higher in urban areas.</li>
<li>Log at one-minute intervals for at least a full school day, preferably several days.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 3: Use the decay method (preferred)</h2>
<p>When students leave at lunch or the end of the day, CO₂ generation in the room stops. If the air handler keeps running normally, CO₂ falls exponentially toward the outdoor level. The rate of fall gives the outdoor air change rate directly, without needing to know how much CO₂ the students produced.</p>
<p class="formula">ACH = ln[(C1 − Co) ÷ (C2 − Co)] ÷ t</p>
<p>where C1 is the indoor concentration at the start of the decay period, C2 at the end, Co is outdoor concentration, all in ppm, and t is the elapsed time in hours.</p>
<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>A classroom of 30 ft × 30 ft × 10 ft (9,000 ft³, 255 m³) empties at 3:00 p.m. Readings: C1 = 1,400 ppm at 3:05, C2 = 700 ppm at 3:50, outdoor Co = 420 ppm. Elapsed time 45 minutes = 0.75 h.</p>
<p class="formula">ACH = ln[(1,400 − 420) ÷ (700 − 420)] ÷ 0.75 = ln(980 ÷ 280) ÷ 0.75 = ln(3.5) ÷ 0.75 = 1.253 ÷ 0.75 = 1.67 ACH</p>
<p class="formula">Outdoor air CFM = ACH × Volume ÷ 60 = 1.67 × 9,000 ÷ 60 = 250 cfm</p>
<p>For 28 students and one teacher, that is about 8.6 cfm (4.1 L/s) per person, which you then compare with the design breathing-zone requirement for that classroom.</p>
<p class="note">Use a fitted curve rather than two points if you can. Plot ln(C − Co) against time; the slope of the best-fit line is −ACH. This reduces the effect of sensor noise.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Cross-check with the steady-state method</h2>
<p>During a long class with stable occupancy, CO₂ may level off. At steady state, generation equals removal:</p>
<p class="formula">Outdoor air per person (cfm) = G ÷ (Cs − Co) × 10^6</p>
<p>where G is the CO₂ generation per person in cfm and Cs the steady indoor concentration. Generation depends strongly on age, body size and activity; published values for children are lower than for adults. Choose a value from a recognized reference such as ASHRAE literature for the students' age group and activity.</p>
<p>Two cautions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Many classrooms never reach steady state within a 45 to 60 minute period, particularly when air change rates are low. A rising curve that is still climbing at the end of the period will overstate ventilation if treated as steady.</li>
<li>Generation rate uncertainty can easily be 20% to 30%, which carries directly into the result.</li>
</ul>
<p>For these reasons, use steady state as a check on the decay result, not as the primary method.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Interpret the result</h2>
<ol>
<li>Compare measured outdoor airflow with the design value. A shortfall of more than about 20% is worth investigating.</li>
<li>Check whether the air handler was in its normal occupied mode during the decay; if the unit shut down after classes, the decay measures infiltration only.</li>
<li>Look at the outdoor air damper position, the outdoor airflow station reading (if installed) and VAV or constant-airflow terminal status for the room.</li>
<li>Remember that decay measures outdoor air reaching the room, including any infiltration and transfer air. In a VAV system with a common outdoor air fraction, it reflects the mixed air outdoor fraction times the room's supply airflow.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Step 6: Report and follow up</h2>
<p>Report the method, logger details, time periods, calculated air change rate, outdoor air per person and comparison with design. If ventilation is low, typical causes at the AHU level include outdoor air dampers not opening to minimum, dirty filters reducing total airflow, or VAV boxes throttled below minimum. The diagnostic approach is in <a href="<?= url('guides/classroom-comfort-complaints-troubleshooting') ?>">troubleshooting classroom comfort complaints</a>. For a full assessment of design outdoor air requirements, see <a href="<?= url('guides/ashrae-62-1-ventilation-rate-procedure') ?>">the ASHRAE 62.1 Ventilation Rate Procedure</a>.</p>
<?php guide_end([
    ['How do you measure classroom ventilation with CO2?', 'Log CO2 indoors and outdoors, then use the decay after students leave to calculate air changes per hour from the natural logarithm of the concentration ratio divided by time. Multiply by room volume to get outdoor airflow.'],
    ['Is the CO2 decay method more accurate than steady state?', 'Usually yes, because it does not depend on an assumed CO2 generation rate and many classrooms never reach steady state during a class period. Steady state is a useful cross-check.'],
    ['What CO2 level means a classroom is well ventilated?', 'There is no single CO2 threshold in ASHRAE 62.1. The level depends on occupancy, generation rate and outdoor air per person, so compare calculated outdoor air with the design rate rather than relying on a fixed ppm number.'],
